	One objection already raised against Nowak's argument has to do with concerns of linguistic transfer over time; call this the generational transfer objection. The generational transfer objection is similar to the critique that I will offer of Nowak's view, so it will be useful to discuss the objection here as it is originally articulated in his paper.
	The issue raised by the generational transfer objection, in general terms, is that considerations regarding social identity and autonomy possible only in one's first language may generate reasons to protect current speakers' access to their first language communities, but these reasons do not necessarily translate into reasons why we should ensure that future speakers continue to speak any particular language as opposed to another. Whichever language future speakers are raised in will be their first language- so long as all speakers retain access to whatever their first language happens to be, it does not then matter whether a people's first language ends up changing across different generations. One reviewer puts forward a hypothetical scenario (involving specifically English and French) of a linguistic assimilative policy enacted on a community, which is carried out in such a way that it never leaves any member of any generation without lifelong access to her first language, but that nevertheless eventually achieves a full shift of the first language in that community to the assimilative target language. The challenge, then, is that taking into account only those reasons for linguistic preservation that Nowak has given, such a linguistic shift would be an acceptable outcome as long as it is done so that it does not at any point in time violate any speaker's interest in being able to keep speaking her first language.
	It is useful to reproduce Nowak's response to the generational transfer objection here:
	"...Even if we set aside questions about whether there could be defensible reasons for implementing a curriculum expressly designed to assimilate a linguistic minority, it is hardly clear that the kids in this case will be ok as French fades and disappears. Of course, someone raised as a fully-bilingual speaker of French and English would be better off in a future society in which only English is spoken than a monolingual French speaker would be, or than an adult student of English as a second language. The bilingual person, after all, still has access to a linguistic community in which she counts as a fully-empowered member.
	If these bilingual students are raised as native French speakers by Francophone parents, [...] there are things these students can do when speaking French that they cannot do when speaking English, [...and] these people will end up with a substantial interest in preserving access to their Francophone communities. Without such access, they will be unable to be recognized, as it were, as the people they are. Since every generation will have a significant interest in sharing a native language with their children, and children an interest in sharing their parents' language, the kinds of claims to which linguistic identity gives rise are likely to endure through time." (26)
	The details of the original objector's English-French case are not reproduced in Nowak's essay, so it is not clear what the envisioned hypothetical assimilative process is actually like. Presumably, judging from Nowak's response, it involves an intermediary stage in which certain generations are bilingual in French and English, perhaps with certain generations being more or less proficient in either language.
	The first thing I would like to note is that Nowak's response here again reflects the two entwined throughlines of his view, namely that 1) no language can be traded in for another without losing unique speech-act affordances belonging to the language being given up, and that 2) speakers enjoy a command over their first language(s) that usually cannot be found with their second languages, if they have any. In discussing the case of bilingual individuals, it is no problem for Nowak's view that people can find themselves with more than one first language. For, these two aspects of the view affirm that a bilingual individual has an interest in keeping access to her first language community, and if there happens to be more than one of these for her, it can be argued that she must maintain access to both, as neither language she speaks can be dispensed with in favor of the other without losing out on irreplaceable speech-act space. This is to say that in this case, both languages would enjoy protection under Nowak's view due to each one being some speaker's first language (in this case, it is the same individual who speaks both), and that neither language can be traded for the other.
	The bilingualism case is helpful here as it allows us to see these two parts of Nowak's view- these two senses in which languages can be "untradeable"- as being distinct from one another. In monolinguals, it is easier to motivate a striking sense in which a speaker really cannot do without access to her first language. Since a monolingual's first language is the only first language she has, if she is cut off from it, she not only loses the uniqueness of her particular language's speech-act space, but at the same time, she also loses all the linguistic materials with which she is maximally fluent. However, in "full" bilingual individuals, when one language is lost, it does not, on the face of things, seem so dire; there is another, albeit different, language in which she can continue to exercise first-language mastery. So, the second aspect of the view does the rest of the lifting here to motivate a continued access to both languages, rather than merely one on the basis of fluency alone. This clarity in the distinction between the two aspects of Nowak's view does naturally invite some curiosity as to whether one of these claims takes priority over the other or whether either, or which, is more central to his view. I will return to this question later on.
	What is underscored far less in Nowak's response is the appeal to the interest that families have in maintaining linguistic continuity across generations. Such an interest seems to be considered an independent or accidental factor, but one that will naturally take care of the generational transfer problem, if it is not interfered with.
	While, all things being equal, the familial interest in generational linguistic cohesion likely drives generational trasmission of native languages well enough under typical conditions, I think that once a non-ideal scenario comes to pass, Nowak's justifications for language preservation become progressively less and less relevant in such circumstances. To make matters worse, I think that in order to motivate language revitalization, at any stage or metric of linguistic "health," it is required to disregard some of the considerations that Nowak outlines that are meant to be in favor of language preservation. At the very least, I hope to show that the view as it is currently stated will require some revision in order to accommodate a wider spectrum of projects and goals related to language preservation. To begin with, I will first posit three cases of language endangerment, abstractly and hypothetically discussed, which I argue Nowak's view as it stands cannot motivate language revitalization as a remedy for. I turn to the first of these now.